[Music]  if you're building an app there's a good  chance that at some point you'll want to  convert your users into paying customers  the most popular api for doing just that  is stripe  it's the payment solution behind a huge  number of successful startups and  companies  like twitch squarespace and shopify just  to name a few  stripe has multiple javascript libraries  that you can implement in the front end  to easily collect credit card  information from your customers  in today's video you'll learn how to use  both stripe checkout and stripe elements  in an angular application  if you're new here like and subscribe  and you can grab the full source code on  fireship io  now the first thing i want to point out  is that this video is only covering the  front end implementation  that involves securely collecting credit  card information from the user and then  validating it with stripe but it does  not involve creating the actual charge  that will collect money from the user  for that you need a back-end  implementation and if you're using  firebase that means firebase cloud  functions luckily i just released my  full-length stripe payments master  course and it will show you how to  implement and test everything on the  back end as well as integrate it with  firebase  the course will show you how to build a  full stack payment solution similar to  the one i have on fireship io that  handle single charges subscriptions  coupons and more but enough of that  shameless plug let's get into our  angular application and start writing  some code  we're going to start with stripe  checkout which is very easy to implement  and does almost all the front-end work  for you when the user clicks on this buy  button  it will trigger this pop-up modal which  will then collect their credit card  information  and do all the validation directly in  that modal this is great as a drop-in  solution  but it doesn't give you a lot of  flexibility to customize the styling or  the entire checkout experience  now at this point i want to make it  clear that i'm using firebase in this  project  it's not at all required to work with  stripe and angular but i want to show  you what a real charge looks like  so in order to do that i've implemented  user authentication in this app as well  as firebase callable functions  as you can see i have those dependencies  in my app module via the angularfire  library  one other thing you'll probably want to  do is install the types for either  stripe checkout or stripe.js  just so you have autocomplete in your  ide as you're writing your code  from there we'll go into angular to the  index.html file  and then add the script for stripe  checkout stripe recommends including  this as a script tag in the html as  opposed to including it in your  javascript bundle  now i've created a component called  checkout and the first important thing  i'll do  is declare a namespace for stripe  checkout in the top of this file  and you can strong type it if you  installed the typings that i showed you  earlier then i'm adding my auth service  and angularfire functions to the  constructor  that's not required for stripe checkout  and you can replace that with nothing or  with your own logic  now there's a good chance you have more  than one product so we'll add input  properties here to pass in an amount  and also a description for the product  being sold then we'll declare a handler  which is the actual modal window that  the user will enter their credit card  details in  then i'm also going to show you a  confirmation of an actual charge from  stripe and we'll set a loading state to  false  we can define the handler during ng on  init and we do that by calling stripe  checkout configure  and at this point you'll need to pass it  your stripe publishable key  you can find that by going to the stripe  dashboard under developers api  keys there's a bunch of different  options that you can pass in here to  customize things like the avatar image  and things like that  but the important thing is that you  define a callback for either the source  or the token  when the user enters their payment  details they will be securely  transferred to stripe  and then striper will spawn back to your  front end with a tokenized version of  the payment source or credit card  so the code right here will run after  the user has entered their card details  stripe has validated that card and now  we can use it to actually create a  charge on our own server  there's quite a few things that you'll  want to do here first we'll set the  loading state to true  and then we'll get the currently  authenticated user the backend that we  build on the stripe master course is  based on firebase callable functions  this is a special type of http function  that gives us the context of the  currently authenticated user  this means we can make requests to our  backend without having to set  authorization headers manually  in this case we just need to pass a  source and an amount and our backend  will charge the card that will take a  second or two and then we'll go ahead  and set the loading state to false  and that's all it takes to create a  charge from our back end the only thing  left to do is create a method here  called checkout that will open the  handler when the user clicks a button  you can also add additional details here  for example you might want to add the  user's email address  and the amount and description of the  product we can also close the handler  manually  and we'll want to do that if the user  clicks the back button by adding a host  listener to the window pop state event  in the html we'll just go ahead and  create a button and then we'll bind the  click event to that checkout handler  now when creating the amount for a  stripe charge it will always be in the  currency's smallest denomination which  for us dollars would be pennies  and that's why i divide the amount by  100 so we have the actual dollar amount  to show in the ui  and then i'll add ngf here for the  loading state and also for the  confirmation that we get back from our  back end  now when a customer goes online to buy  some meat from our store they have a  modal to enter their credit card details  that is not only secure but also looks  pretty nice  after the card is captured it will run  the code in that callback  it first puts the app in the loading  state for a few seconds then it responds  with a charge object from stripe  which has all kinds of information about  the charge itself like the id the amount  and stuff like that  so overall that was very easy and we  created a real charge in the striped  testing environment  but the main drawback to stripe checkout  is that we don't have a lot of control  over the checkout experience  we can change the logo and some of the  options but everything else is basically  hard-coded into that modal  luckily stripe has another library  called stripe elements that allows you  to build checkout forms without having  to do everything from scratch  we use stripe elements on fireship and  as you can see here we get the same  validation and security that we get with  the checkout form  however it is a lot easier to customize  and as you can see here it easily adapts  to multiple themes  and it's really not that much more  difficult to set up so first we're going  to go into the index.html  and this time import stripe.js version  3.  we'll declare a global variable for  stripe and this time the typings are  under the stripe namespace  now the component will start off just  like the previous one did we pass in the  amount and the product description  but then we need to grab a dom element  to attach the payment form to  so viewchild will be looking for a  template reference called card element  which we'll create once we get to the  html  now we have a few things to declare here  we have the instance of stripe elements  itself  we have the card that represents the  actual card details and then any card  errors  and then we'll also have a loading state  and a confirmation from our back end  now you can initialize stripe with your  publishable key and then stripe elements  will give you access to a bunch of  different form fields  if you just have a generic credit card  form you can create the card element and  that's all you need that will give you a  nice single line for the card itself but  you can break that down into multiple  fields if you want from here we'll go  ahead and mount the card to the actual  dom element which we do by calling card  element native element in angular  at this point we need to attach an event  listener to changes to the card form  in this example we're just going to  display the card errors but you might  also want to disable the submit button  if the card is not valid  when it comes to stripe elements we need  to handle the actual submission of the  payment form  unlike the previous example where we  defined a source callback this time  we'll actually call stripe create source  and then handle it inside this async  function if there are any errors then  we'll go ahead and show those in the  view  otherwise we're going to run the same  exact logic that we ran in that source  handler which involves getting the  current user  calling the callable function to create  the charge in the back end and then  setting the loading state to false  now we can switch over to the html and  first we'll create a form element  and then we'll bind the handle form  method to the submission of this form  then we'll create a div with hashtag  card element that creates the template  variable that we can actually mount to  and then we'll also display the card  errors here as well and that's all it  takes to build a validated and secure  payment form with stripe and angular  now we can just drop this component  anywhere in our angular app and we have  a quick and easy way for users to pay  for products or sign up for  subscriptions  i'm going to go ahead and wrap things up  there if this video helped you please  like and subscribe and if you're serious  about building a payment system with  stripe consider becoming a pro member at  fireship io i'm leaving a discount code  down in the description  and if you have any questions make sure  to leave me a comment thanks for  watching and i will talk to you soon  oh

